{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.OneDim.TLIsXXZChain","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.OneDim.TLIsXXZChain","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.MeanInequalities","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.eq_one_of_anisotropy_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.eq_one_of_anisotropy_le","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L137-L144","doc":"Contrapositive form: an anisotropy at or below `1` forces `q = 1`, i.e. the\nundeformed (classical) point. There is no real `q ≠ 1` giving a massless chain,\nso the massless phase is unreachable from a real substrate parameter — it\nrequires `|q| = 1`, which the corpus's `q₀ ≈ 1.10998` is not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.one_lt_anisotropy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.one_lt_anisotropy","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L113-L135","doc":"**The substrate is in the massive (Ising-like) regime.** For real `q > 0`\nwith `q ≠ 1`, the XXZ anisotropy exceeds `1`.\n\nThis is what makes Bethe strings *stable bound states* rather than scattering\nstates: `Δ > 1` is the gapped, Ising-like phase of the XXZ chain, whereas\n`|Δ| < 1` is the massless phase where no such bound states survive.\n\nIt carries **no calibration input and no free parameter**: the conclusion\nfollows from `q ≠ 1` alone, so it is forced by the substrate being a genuine\ndeformation rather than fitted to reproduce anything. The numerical value at\n`q₀` is `Δ - 1 ≈ 5.448e-3`, agreeing between the two documented pins to\n~1.2 ppb (measured in the probe cited above) — so the verdict does not depend\non which pin is used. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.tlLoop_add_heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.tlLoop_add_heckeH","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L95-L102","doc":"`δ` and `h` determine `q`: their half-sum is `q`. Records that no\ninformation is lost in passing from the Hecke presentation to the chain\npresentation — the map is invertible, not a projection. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.xxzAnisotropy_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.xxzAnisotropy_eq","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L92-L93","doc":"The anisotropy is the half-sum, stated so downstream files need not unfold. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.twoInv_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.twoInv_eq","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L83-L90","doc":"**Content of the `|↓↑⟩` diagonal entry**, the partner of `twoMul_eq`: the\nsame two quantities recombine to `2q⁻¹` with the opposite sign on `h`. The two\ntogether are the whole non-trivial content of the two-site matrix identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.twoMul_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.twoMul_eq","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L74-L81","doc":"**Content of the `|↑↓⟩` diagonal entry.** The anisotropy and the Hecke\nparameter recombine to `2q`: this is why the TL generator's `(↑↓,↑↓)` entry is\n`q` and not something requiring a further constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.heckeH","kind":"def","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L69-L72","doc":"The Hecke parameter `h = q - q⁻¹`, the right-hand side of the Hecke\nrelation `σ² = h·σ + 1`. It is the coefficient of the boundary term that makes\nthe chain `U_q(sl₂)`-symmetric rather than `SU(2)`-symmetric. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.xxzAnisotropy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.xxzAnisotropy","kind":"def","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L66-L67","doc":"The XXZ anisotropy, `Δ = δ/2`. Over `ℝ` with `q > 0` this is `cosh (log q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneDim.tlLoop","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneDim.tlLoop","kind":"def","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneDim/TLIsXXZChain.lean#L61-L64","doc":"The Temperley–Lieb loop weight `δ = q + q⁻¹`, i.e. the balanced quantum\ninteger `[2]_q`. This is the norm `⟨w, w⟩` of the `q`-deformed singlet\n`w = q^{1/2}|↑↓⟩ - q^{-1/2}|↓↑⟩`, hence the scalar in `e² = δ • e`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}